+# Contributing to Hello-World
+
+Thank you for considering a contribution! This guide explains how to file an issue, how to open a pull request, and what we expect from every contribution. If anything here is unclear, please ask by opening an issue.
+
+## Ways to Contribute
+
+- Report bugs or problems you have run into.
+- Suggest ideas and improvements.
+- Improve documentation, including this guide.
+- Submit code or documentation changes via pull requests.
+
+## Filing an Issue
+
+1. Search the existing issues at <https://github.com/octocat/Hello-World/issues>, including closed ones, to see if your problem or idea has already been reported.
+2. If it has not, open a new issue with a short, descriptive title.
+3. In the description, include:
+   - What you expected to happen.
+   - What actually happened, with the steps to reproduce it.
+   - Relevant environment details, such as your operating system and the tools or versions involved.
+
+Issues are public. Please never include secrets, credentials, or other sensitive data in an issue report.
+
+## Opening a Pull Request
+
+1. Fork the repository to your own GitHub account.
+2. Clone your fork:
+
+   ```bash
+   git clone https://github.com/<username>/Hello-World.git
+   cd Hello-World
+   ```
+
+3. Create a branch for your change:
+
+   ```bash
+   git checkout -b my-contribution
+   ```
+
+4. Make your change and commit it with a clear, descriptive message:
+
+   ```bash
+   git add <changed-files>
+   git commit -m "Short summary of the change"
+   ```
+
+5. Push the branch to your fork:
+
+   ```bash
+   git push origin my-contribution
+   ```
+
+6. Open a pull request from your branch to the `master` branch of `octocat/Hello-World`.
+7. Describe what the change does and why it is needed, and link the related issue with `Fixes #<issue number>`. Every pull request should reference the issue it resolves.
+
+## Expectations for Contributions
+
+- Communicate respectfully and constructively in issues, reviews, and pull requests.
+- Keep changes small and focused on a single purpose.
+- Follow the existing style of the repository's files.
+
+This repository has no test suite, linter, or build system. Maintainers review changes by reading them, so a clear commit message and a well-described pull request are what make a contribution easy to review.
+
+## Questions?
+
+If you have a question that does not fit an issue, open one anyway and label it as a question. The issue tracker is the fastest way to reach the maintainers.
